card.statement:
The transforming matrix $S(X)$ in <<ref:tef>> does not always can be taken as an analytic function of the entries of $X$: if $A_{\text{can}}=I_1$, then $A_{\text{can}} +{\cal D}\langle
A_{\text{can}}\rangle =I_1+[\bullet]$ and by Theorem <<ref:teo2>> there exist a neighborhood $U$ of $0$ and continuous mappings $s:U\to \mathbb C$ and $\varphi: U\to i\mathbb R$ such that $s(0)=1$ and

\begin{equation*}
[s(x)]^*[1+x][s(x)]=[1+\varphi(x)]
\end{equation*}

for all $x\in U$. Klimenko <<cite:klim>> proved that $s$ cannot be an analytic function. In contrast to this, the transforming matrices for matrices under similarity <<cite:arn>>, matrix pencils under strict equivalence <<cite:kag>>, and matrices under congruence <<cite:f_s>> always can be taken analytic.
